e seats  purchased however it's not the same for  business lights business traveller is  always booked last-minute and as a  result their prices are always soar in  half but if you booked a trip from  London to Frankfurt a few months in  advance you might be able to get an  amazing  deal while your fellow passengers next  to you will have to purchase a ticket  with a price twice as high important  days of the week you've probably heard  that the price can vary depending on  which day you're purchasing your ticket  according to a survey done by the  airline reporting corporation the most  expensive data by flight tickets is  Friday while Tuesdays and Thursdays are  the best for lowering costs these prices  were first determined by marketing  managers who were responsible for flash  sales and offers but now they are  automated and unified across most  airlines the important days of the week  will also vary depending on the location  of the airline for US airlines Tuesdays  at midnight tend to produce the best  search results but it's not the same for  other airlines around the world other  factors but there are many more factors  that determine the price of your plane  seat if a similar airline has cheaper  seats for that same route everyone else  will have to adjust to make sure their  planes still get fully booked  however for destinations with very  little competition the prices are  typically more expensive and hardly ever  drop of course the longer the flight the  more expensive your ticket will be your  ticket can also become expensive if the  fuel prices become higher as the airline  still has to make some kind of profit  from flying you to your destination  speaking of destinations sometimes a  connecting flight can turn out to be  much cheaper than a non-stop destination  without any lay overs seats apply so  let's have a look at how the airline's  price their seats your neighbor might  have paid a completely different price  to yours even though you're on the same  flight and sometimes they can even  charge completely different fares for  the identical seat  this is known as yield management a  strategy that separates customers into  two categories the first is those that  are willing to pay any amount for their  ticket as we explained before people  like business travellers and celebrities  typically don't care about the price but  if the airline only relies on the first  group they might never be able to fill  as much of the plane as possible here's  when the seating prices start to change  the second group are travelers who are  looking for a much cheaper option why do  the same seat prices  typically the airline will cater to high  budget passengers first and depending on  the demand the strategy will change to  sell as many seats as possible this is  precisely why you might see a  significant drop in price for your  chosen see the strategy allows the  airlines to fly full planes and make as  much profit as possible airlines have  over a dozen fares for identical seats  and they all run at the same time in  order to make sure all these prices  adjust accordingly the airlines run  software programs that will monitor  flights and booking patterns that way  they are able to oversee the behavior of  their customers and predict how when and  why will a person book their plane  ticket what about low-cost airlines if  you live in Europe you're probably  familiar with airlines like easyJet and  Ryanair which will sell flight tickets  for as low as 10 euros in one direction  often described as tickets that are  cheaper than a taxi these airlines work  on a different low luxury strategy that  allows them to only provide the basic  necessities to their customers from  seats that don't recline to the lack of  back pockets allows their crew to spend  less time on maintenance and cleaning  and therefore reduce total costs this is  extremely convenient for shorter flights  and frequent routes but that's not the  only thing that helps low-cost airlines  make profit all while making their  flights it gets completely affordable  type of aircraft low-cost airlines are  also known to buy their aircraft in bulk  which can often affect the price of the  actual affairs they also buy specific  types of airplanes and are therefore  able to reduce their staff training  costs to only those aircrafts this helps  them save money while also budgeting  enough for the expensive fuel and other  costs that come with being an airline by  reducing costs on practically everything  European airlines like easyJet and  Ryanair are able to offer competitive  that can't be beat and since empty seats  cost money low-cost airlines are able to  fully booked most of their flights  without any problems  automated process many of these pricing  systems are totally automated  which is why savvy travelers usually  know how to find cheap flights you  should also always compare your prices  as much as possible to find better deals  you never knew about websites like sky  scanner and the mundo allow you to  easily see all possible fair prices for  your chosen destinations these websites  expose all strategies and make it easy  to find a flight for a much better price  so next time you're booking a flight  ticket remember how Airlines price their  flights and see if you can get a better  deal good luck how do you find cheap  flight deals make sure to let us know in  the comments and that's it for our video  for more videos like this one check out  10 things you should never do on an  airplane see you next time  [Music]

